\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8.25}{\ignorespaces Heap allocation over the default scope of 463 patterns and 11\,395 entries. \emph  {Total allocated} counts every allocation, including memory later freed, and measures allocator pressure. \emph  {Avg/entry} divides that total by the parser's own measured-entry count, since total alone scales with how many entries a parser completes and the standard variants exclude their timeouts. \emph  {Max live peak} is the highest live-byte high-water mark within a single call and measures the footprint a deployment would see. Total and peak are different quantities: total is cumulative and can exceed the live peak, because freed memory is counted in the former but not the latter. Timed-out entries are excluded from all three, so each is a lower bound on the standard variants' true figure.}}{70}{table.caption.84}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:memory}{{8.25}{70}{Heap allocation over the default scope of 463 patterns and 11\,395 entries. \emph {Total allocated} counts every allocation, including memory later freed, and measures allocator pressure. \emph {Avg/entry} divides that total by the parser's own measured-entry count, since total alone scales with how many entries a parser completes and the standard variants exclude their timeouts. \emph {Max live peak} is the highest live-byte high-water mark within a single call and measures the footprint a deployment would see. Total and peak are different quantities: total is cumulative and can exceed the live peak, because freed memory is counted in the former but not the latter. Timed-out entries are excluded from all three, so each is a lower bound on the standard variants' true figure}{table.caption.84}{}}
